WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.454
Start on the current Home screen.

2
00:00:02.454 --> 00:00:08.389
A display name and one of the four workflow labels start the fictional
demo workspace.

3
00:00:08.389 --> 00:00:15.907
The field attributes later human actions; it is not authentication, and
choosing a role does not grant real-world authority.

4
00:00:15.907 --> 00:00:21.050
We will use the reordered left navigation to visit every current product
page.

5
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.554
Case Queue is the operational starting point.

6
00:00:26.554 --> 00:00:33.998
Its summary cards separate total alerts, open work, source-closed labels,
escalation counts, and High severity.

7
00:00:33.998 --> 00:00:40.947
These are stored fictional workflow labels, not proof that money is lawful
or unlawful.

8
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.314
Scroll slowly to the filters and table.

9
00:00:46.314 --> 00:00:58.790
Search customer name narrows the queue; the row then exposes case,
customer, alert type, severity, current status, recorded rule labels, and
an action to inspect the case.

10
00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:02.486
A status and an action have distinct meanings.

11
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.852
The filtered queue demonstrates the same review flow with a smaller result
set.

12
00:01:08.852 --> 00:01:11.054
An open case can be investigated.

13
00:01:11.054 --> 00:01:22.066
A closed or reviewed case instead retains an Evidence path, so the user
can inspect the support without treating the label as an invitation to
change a completed human decision.

14
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.490
Investigation Workspace begins with a selected case and a repeatable
saved-report mode.

15
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:36.704
Run investigation replays a historical snapshot or the calculated mode can
evaluate the current fictional rows.

16
00:01:36.704 --> 00:01:40.311
Neither mode records a human decision by itself.

17
00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.257
After the report is built, Evidence confidence appears prominently.

18
00:01:46.257 --> 00:01:53.682
Here the support score is 45 out of 100, with Low support and four known
limitations.

19
00:01:53.682 --> 00:02:01.571
This is a transparent evidence-coverage check, not a probability of crime,
money laundering, or the recommended outcome.

20
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.911
Continue down the report to the case context and Evidence and Findings.

21
00:02:07.911 --> 00:02:11.936
The screen preserves its source-data warning and identifies review-window
facts.

22
00:02:11.936 --> 00:02:17.572
It makes the limitation visible before any person is asked to make a
decision.

23
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.114
Evidence and RAG is where citations become inspectable records.

24
00:02:23.114 --> 00:02:31.181
Its compact flow describes choosing a case, reading exact source text and
chunks, checking coverage, and testing retrieval.

25
00:02:31.181 --> 00:02:37.456
This is the evidence workspace, not a claim that a cited record
establishes wrongdoing.

26
00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.260
Scroll into Case records.

27
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:48.891
The selected case shows its indexed passages, identity-record limitations,
original-file count, and unmatched counterparties.

28
00:02:48.891 --> 00:02:56.521
The page explicitly distinguishes a generated record or dataset field from
independently verified evidence.

29
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.048
The floating InvestigateIQ Copilot opens on the same page, keeping the
selected case visible.

30
00:03:04.048 --> 00:03:12.147
Because this capture is a non-submitting demonstration, it asks the viewer
to enter a workspace before asking a question.

31
00:03:12.147 --> 00:03:18.541
The Copilot is a bounded explanation tool; it never selects or submits the
human action.

32
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.704
Rule Lab is a separate evidence tab.

33
00:03:22.704 --> 00:03:33.577
It explains the alert-rule metadata and preview boundary, so a red or
matched condition is read as a review signal—not a verdict and not a
replacement for source evidence.

34
00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.531
Fictional Intake shows the separate calculated packet workflow.

35
00:03:38.531 --> 00:03:51.041
This deliberately generated example is visibly different from an imported
alert: its rows, source text, and evidence gaps are labeled as fictional
rather than presented as original banking records.

36
00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:58.789
Source and chunks lets an investigator open the source document behind a
cited guidance passage.

37
00:03:58.789 --> 00:04:07.162
The document and its chunks are shown separately, which makes the
retrieval path reviewable rather than hiding it inside a generated answer.

38
00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:15.102
As we continue to scroll, exact chunks and their metadata stay connected
to their source.

39
00:04:15.102 --> 00:04:19.519
A chunk is a searchable excerpt with an identifier and scope.

40
00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:25.541
It is useful for traceability, but its presence does not independently
establish a customer fact.

41
00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:30.713
Search the index makes the retrieval method visible.

42
00:04:30.713 --> 00:04:39.797
It searches the stored fictional sources and case passages; results are
leads for inspection, not unverified answers presented as facts.

43
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:49.929
The detailed result area lets the investigator compare result text, scope,
and source identifier before relying on a passage.

44
00:04:49.929 --> 00:04:54.887
This is why the product shows the evidence boundary alongside the result.

45
00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:01.729
Method and code documents how records, chunks, retrieval, and checks
connect.

46
00:05:01.729 --> 00:05:10.182
It is included so a reviewer can understand the product’s method rather
than treating the UI as a black box.

47
00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.788
Continue through the implementation notes slowly.

48
00:05:14.788 --> 00:05:25.621
The documented method is deliberately limited: it can retrieve and trace
supplied fictional material, but cannot verify outside-bank facts or make
a legal finding.

49
00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.595
Compliance Queue contains cases an investigator has explicitly escalated.

50
00:05:32.595 --> 00:05:38.615
It is a second human-review step, separate from the investigator’s
decision panel.

51
00:05:38.615 --> 00:05:43.632
Nothing on this screen files a report with a regulator.

52
00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:59.614
The Compliance outcomes are distinguished in the queue and audit history:
acknowledge means reviewed with no further action; return sends the case
back for more information; referred onward records that a referral was
made by a human.

53
00:05:59.614 --> 00:06:02.906
The labels preserve the next state of the case.

54
00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:08.885
Analytics starts with descriptive summaries of the fictional dataset.

55
00:06:08.885 --> 00:06:19.032
Read each title and axis as a count or share of stored labels—not as a
prediction, fraud score, productivity rating, or probability of crime.

56
00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.943
Here the graphs are fully in view.

57
00:06:23.943 --> 00:06:28.851
Alerts by current status uses number of cases on the horizontal axis.

58
00:06:28.851 --> 00:06:32.941
High-severity share by typology uses percentage on the horizontal axis.

59
00:06:32.941 --> 00:06:39.076
The explanatory text below each graph states the unit and what it does not
measure.

60
00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:48.348
As the page scrolls further, the remaining charts show stored records over
time and recorded human actions.

61
00:06:48.348 --> 00:06:53.051
A monthly alert line counts fictional records for each time bucket.

62
00:06:53.051 --> 00:06:59.036
Human-action charts count saved actions; they do not measure final
outcomes or staff performance.

63
00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.943
Global Search is read-only.

64
00:07:02.943 --> 00:07:14.589
A user can look up a fictional customer, account, transaction reference,
or case identifier, then use a matching record to regain context in
Investigation Workspace.

65
00:07:14.589 --> 00:07:18.782
Search locates records; it does not reach a conclusion.

66
00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:29.076
Project and Team names the confirmed roster and makes the product’s
planning responsibilities visible by person.

67
00:07:29.076 --> 00:07:36.072
This section uses names only, without email addresses, and does not imply
production permissions.

68
00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.054
The open role guide explains the four workflow roles.

69
00:07:42.054 --> 00:07:55.741
The Investigator reviews evidence and records an accountable action; the
Team Lead coordinates; the Compliance Officer conducts second review; the
Admin maintains fictional guidance and rules after a separate passcode
gate.

70
00:07:55.741 --> 00:07:58.390
Labels do not authenticate a user.

71
00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.938
Admin Knowledge Base is intentionally shown in its locked state.

72
00:08:04.938 --> 00:08:09.797
The video does not expose, store, or automate a passcode.

73
00:08:09.797 --> 00:08:15.627
The boundary is visible: protected source-inspection tools require a
separate authorized unlock.

74
00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:22.005
Admin Rule Config has the same protected boundary.

75
00:08:22.005 --> 00:08:32.309
This concludes the current product tour: InvestigateIQ shows the evidence
trail, the retrieval method, the score’s limitation, and the human
workflow.

76
00:08:32.309 --> 00:08:37.706
It does not make the final decision or file anything automatically.
